-- title:
--   Lemma 4.6: Convex-type modules and antisymplectic involutions
-- statement:
--   All vector spaces and Lie algebras are finite dimensional and real, with their usual Hausdorff topology. An invariant set is invariant under the group generated by $e^{\operatorname{ad}y}$. A closed convex set is pointed when its recession edge is zero. Write $\mathcal O_x=\operatorname{Inn}(\mathfrak g)x$, $\operatorname{co}(x)=\overline{\operatorname{conv}}\mathcal O_x$, and $C_x=\operatorname{cone}(\mathcal O_x)$. Admissibility means existence of a pointed generating invariant closed convex subset.
--
--   Let $\mathfrak l$ be reductive quasihermitian and $\mathfrak s\subseteq\mathfrak l$ semisimple, normalized by a compactly embedded Cartan subalgebra, with every simple ideal hermitian of tube type. Let $(V,\omega)$ be a symplectic $\mathfrak l$-module of convex type. Set $V_{\rm eff}=\operatorname{span}(\mathfrak s.V)$ and $V_{\rm fix}=\{v:\mathfrak s.v=0\}$. Then
--
--   $$V=V_{\rm eff}\oplus^{\perp_\omega}V_{\rm fix},$$
--
--   and both summands are $\mathfrak s$-submodules, with the effective summand symplectic of convex type. For every Euler element $h\in\mathfrak s$ whose $\pm1$ eigenspaces generate $\mathfrak s$, $2\rho(h)$ is an antisymplectic involution on $V_{\rm eff}$. The subalgebra need not be an ideal.
-- source:
--   Elements in pointed invariant cones in Lie algebras and corresponding affine pairs, arXiv:2110.07297v1, PDF p. 21, Lemma 4.6: Convex-type modules and antisymplectic involutions

